WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:09.660
A long time ago in a galaxy far, far away,
George Lucas created Star Wars: A New Hope,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.629
spawning an entire franchise of awesomeness.

00:00:12.629 --> 00:00:16.379
Decades later, we're still learning all kinds
of secrets from behind the scenes.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:20.140
Stabilize your rear deflectors as we scan
the Star Wars saga for some stuff even the

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.510
Jedi Council didn't know.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.670
Number one: IG-88 on tap

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:29.300
As part of the group of bounty hunters hired
by Darth Vader to find the Millennium Falcon

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.820
in Empire Strikes Back, IG-88 looked really
intimidating… but at the same time, he looked

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.240
really familiar.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.090
That's because he was previously seen in A
New Hope, in one of the least likely of places:

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.660
as a Mos Eisley Cantina drink dispenser.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.740
That's right, the same prop pieces used to
create IG-88's head are also scattered behind

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:48.780
the cantina's bar.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.170
R2-D2 and C-3PO were just steps away from
becoming a couple of bickering distilleries.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.739
But IG-88 isn’t the only one with an unlucky
head...

00:00:56.739 --> 00:00:59.460
Number two: Jango hits his head

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.160
We've all seen the Star Wars blooper of the
Stormtrooper accidentally walking into a door

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.460
in A New Hope.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:08.020
George Lucas even went back to add a sound
effect to the mishap during the DVD re-release

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.840
of the original trilogy.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.190
But did you know that Lucas paid homage to
this blooper in Attack of the Clones?

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.770
After the fight between Jango Fett and Obi-Wan
Kenobi, the bounty hunter can be seen fleeing

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:20.400
to the Slave I as a young Boba Fett is firing
up its engines.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.110
During Jango's hasty retreat, you can see
the ship's door clipping his helmet as he

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.810
passes, accompanied by an appropriate sound
effect.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.010
Some argue that Jango didn't actually hit
his head during this blink-and-you-miss-it

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:34.330
moment, but we'd imagine that Lucas would've
closed the door normally if he meant otherwise.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.960
Now we know where that Stormtrooper inherited
his clumsiness.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.680
It’s certainly not the worst thing that
happened to Jango’s head, though.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.119
Number three: Marvel gives Star Wars a hand

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:48.090
Phase Two of the Marvel Cinematic Universe
is filled with tributes to The Empire Strikes

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.090
Back.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:54.560
Specifically, every movie in Phase Two includes
a scene of a character losing an arm, Skywalker-style.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.479
Marvel Studios President Kevin Feige said
to CinemaBlend, "I’m obsessed with Star

00:01:58.479 --> 00:01:59.479
Wars.

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:02.159
It didn’t start out as intentional, but
it became intentional."

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:04.130
Number four: Those darn helmets

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.270
"I can't see a thing in this helmet!"

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.450
You think that explains why the Stormtroopers
are all bad shots?

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.910
Well, that wasn't exactly in the script.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:16.610
In this scene, Mark Hamill was complaining
to Harrison Ford after he thought the cameras

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:17.730
had stopped rolling.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.180
Surprisingly, Lucas decided to keep the line
in the movie.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.370
Just be happy you weren't wearing that Darth
Vader mask, Luke.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.400
Number five: The Force Awakens gets Raided

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.099
We've all seen the awesome cameos packed into
The Force Awakens, but there are a lot more

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:36.360
than you may realize, including appearances
by Bill Hader, Simon Pegg, Ken Leung, Warwick

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.950
Davis, and James Bond himself, Daniel Craig.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.720
For those wondering, Craig played the Stormtrooper
who was Jedi mind tricked by Rey into setting

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:43.970
her free.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.480
There are a ton of Game of Thrones cameos
as well.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.330
We all know Brienne of Tarth actress Gwendoline
Christie plays Captain Phasma, but there are

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.470
plenty of other cameos brought in from the
Seven Kingdoms.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:59.620
Thomas Brodie Sangster, Jessica Henwick, Mark
Stanley, Miltos Yerolemou, and Emun Elliott

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.390
also appear in small roles throughout the
film.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.660
Our favorite Force Awakens cameo comes in
the form of the criminal gang, Kanjiklub.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.690
Fans of the Indonesian action flicks The Raid:
Redemption and The Raid 2 should recognize

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.819
Yayan Ruhian, Iko Uwais, and Cecep Arif Rahman.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:17.440
That's Rama and two of his fiercest opponents
from the franchise!

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.849
Number six: Starkiller vs. Skywalker

00:03:20.849 --> 00:03:24.500
Dedicated Star Wars fans know that George
Lucas originally intended for the protagonist

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.630
of his series to have the last name of "Starkiller."

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:30.980
Of course, Starkiller sounds a bit darker
than Skywalker, so the name was changed.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.010
Eventually, LucasArts could use the Starkiller
name for the protagonist in Star Wars: The

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.010
Force Unleashed.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.379
This video game puts you in the jackboots
of Darth Vader's secret apprentice, ordered

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:44.170
to create a small rebellion against the Empire
to distract the Emperor enough to be overthrown.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.700
These events actually led to the creation
of the Rebel Alliance.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:51.080
Unfortunately, most of the lore outside of
the movies and recent TV series got thrown

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.940
into the Sarlacc Pit once Disney took control
of the series.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:54.940
At least J.J.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.180
Abrams brought the Starkiller name back for
the new Death Star.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.769
We know, it's technically not a Death Star,
but actually a mobile ice planet superweapon.

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:06.000
It's still just a really, really big Death
Star that can blast multiple planets at once

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.480
from across the solar system.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.870
Big difference.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.900
Number seven: Where credit is due

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.780
We all love the iconic "A long time ago in
a galaxy far, far away…" scroll before each

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.949
Star Wars film, but did you ever notice that
there are no opening credits before any of

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:20.379
the movies in the series?

00:04:20.379 --> 00:04:25.719
The Directors Guild of America slapped George
Lucas with a hefty $250,000 fine for not properly

00:04:25.719 --> 00:04:30.099
crediting the director before the film, even
though he wasn't the first person to do this.

00:04:30.099 --> 00:04:34.430
As a result, Lucas severed ties with the guild
like it was a Skywalker's arm.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:35.430
Youch!

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.650
Number eight: Jedi Master Tupac

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.130
Yes, you read that right, and no, we're not
making this up.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.169
Rolling Stone reports the late, great Tupac
Shakur auditioned to star as Mace Windu in

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:46.749
The Phantom Menace.

00:04:46.749 --> 00:04:51.610
Details are a bit shaky, since Tupac was murdered
in September of 1996 and filming on Episode

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.569
I started nine months later.

00:04:53.569 --> 00:04:57.069
Nevertheless, just imagine if "Thug Life"
himself was cast as Mace Windu.

00:04:57.069 --> 00:05:00.509
Tupac could have tossed aside his beef with
the East Coast's rappers and started feuding

00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:01.530
with the Sith instead.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:05.699
While some diehard rap fans believe Tupac
may still be alive and in hiding, there’s

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:11.190
no way that Mace Windu survived that brutal
electrocution and defenestration.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.719
Number nine: The rasp of Leia

00:05:13.719 --> 00:05:17.250
Did you ever watch Return of the Jedi and
wonder why this voice sounds familiar?

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.900
(Star Wars alien voice speaking)

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.461
Surprisingly, that voice was done by the late
actress Pat Welsh.

00:05:23.461 --> 00:05:28.020
While her list of film credits is short, you
might recognize her for one other prominent

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.199
role: E.T.

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:30.499
the Extra-Terrestrial.

00:05:30.499 --> 00:05:34.589
The voice of that adorable, beer-chugging,
candy-eating alien is the same as the bounty

00:05:34.589 --> 00:05:38.180
hunter disguise Leia used when threatening
to suicide bomb Jabba's palace with a thermal

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.180
detonator.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:43.490
Still, as some fans may know, this isn't the
only connection between ET and the Star Wars

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:44.490
saga...

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:45.490
Thanks for watching!

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.330
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:51.779
And leave us a comment to let us know which
obscure Star Wars fact surprised you the most...

